{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon GameLift Streams now supports IAM role credentials for stream sessions - AWS","context":"Amazon GameLift Streams, a service for real-time multiplayer game sessions, now supports assigning IAM roles to stream sessions. This feature allows applications to securely access AWS resources, such as Amazon S3 and DynamoDB, without the need to embed long-lived access keys in application bundles or pass them as environment variables. By passing a RoleArn parameter when starting a stream session, applications receive short-lived, auto-refreshing AWS credentials through the standard AWS SDK credential resolution chain. IAM role misconfigurations are validated at session start, preventing errors during runtime. The Amazon GameLift Streams console allows users to configure IAM roles directly, providing a pre-filled trust policy template for simplified setup. IAM role support for stream sessions is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon GameLift Streams is available. For more information, refer to the Session Credentials Setup section in the Amazon GameLift Streams Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/amazon-gamelift-streams-iam/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS simplifies IAM role creation and setup in service workflows - AWS","context":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) has introduced a new feature that simplifies the creation and configuration of IAM roles directly within service workflows. This new panel appears when performing console tasks related to role configuration, enabling users to set permissions without switching between browser tabs. IAM roles allow for secure AWS cross-service connections using temporary credentials, eliminating the need for hardcoded access keys. The feature is currently available for use with Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda, Amazon EKS, Amazon ECS, AWS Glue, AWS CloudFormation, AWS Key Management Service, AWS Database Migration Service, AWS Systems Manager, AWS Secrets Manager, and AWS IoT Core in the US East (N. Virginia) Region. The feature will gradually become available for additional AWS services and regions. Users can utilize default policies or the simplified statement builder to customize permissions, streamlining resource setup while maintaining IAM role management functionality","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/aws-simplifies-iam-role-creation-and-setup/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify Forecast cross-account RoleArn causes InvalidInputException per AWS docs", "parent_id": "5d411f55-cb4e-42b9-a1ac-9d857c197b9e"}
